      "seed": 264087474,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 899857774,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1778778141730,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Based on the detailed foundations of PWAs, here are the essential main points\ncategorized for clarity. This covers the mindset, design, and technical pillars you\nneed as a developer:1. The Core Philosophy: \"Progressive\"The \"P\" in PWA stands\nfor Progressive Enhancement.Layered approach: Build the core experience with\nstandard HTML/CSS/JS first (so it works for everyone).Feature Detection: Use\ntools like @supports in CSS and object checks in JS to \"unlock\" advanced\nfeatures only if the browser supports them.No Device Detection: Stop guessing\nif a user is on a \"mobile\" or \"desktop\" based on strings; test for the specific\nfeature or screen size instead.2. The Modern Design StrategyForget the old\n\"mobile vs. desktop\" binary. Modern PWA design is about Intrinsic Design:Mobile\nas a Constraint: Use the small screen to force yourself to prioritize essential\ncontent and actions (Focus).The Mini-Mode: PWAs on desktop can be resized\nsmaller than any browser window (as small as 200x100px). You must design for\nthis \"widget-like\" state.Fluidity: Use CSS Grid and Flexbox to create layouts\nthat respond to the content’s needs, not just fixed pixels.Foldables & Hybrids:\nBe ready for devices that change state (like a phone that unfolds into a\ntablet).3. Performance as a FoundationPerformance isn't just a \"plus\"; it's a\nrequirement. Google measures this through Core Web Vitals:LCP (Largest\nContentful Paint): How fast the main content loads.INP (Interaction to Next\nPaint): How fast the UI reacts when a user clicks/taps.CLS (Cumulative Layout\nShift): Ensuring elements don't \"jump around\" while loading (Visual Stability).4.\nIntegration & ReliabilityA PWA bridges the gap between web and\nnative:Standalone Experience: Once installed, it should look and feel like an app\n(no browser search bar, custom title bar colors).Offline-First: Use Service\nWorkers to ensure the app doesn't break when the network fails.Platform\nIntegration: Accessing the file system, contacts, or hardware APIs when\navailable.Summary Checklist for your Projects:PillarAction ItemDesignPrioritize\ncontent first, then add layout layers.ResponsiveTest for \"Mini-Mode\" and\nfoldable viewports.TechnicalImplement Feature Detection instead of UA\nsniffing.PerformanceOptimize for LCP, INP, and CLS scores.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Based on the detailed foundations of PWAs, here are the essential main points categorized for clarity. This covers the mindset, design, and technical pillars you need as a developer:1. The Core Philosophy: \"Progressive\"The \"P\" in PWA stands for Progressive Enhancement.Layered approach: Build the core experience with standard HTML/CSS/JS first (so it works for everyone).Feature Detection: Use tools like @supports in CSS and object checks in JS to \"unlock\" advanced features only if the browser supports them.No Device Detection: Stop guessing if a user is on a \"mobile\" or \"desktop\" based on strings; test for the specific feature or screen size instead.2. The Modern Design StrategyForget the old \"mobile vs. desktop\" binary. Modern PWA design is about Intrinsic Design:Mobile as a Constraint: Use the small screen to force yourself to prioritize essential content and actions (Focus).The Mini-Mode: PWAs on desktop can be resized smaller than any browser window (as small as 200x100px). You must design for this \"widget-like\" state.Fluidity: Use CSS Grid and Flexbox to create layouts that respond to the content’s needs, not just fixed pixels.Foldables & Hybrids: Be ready for devices that change state (like a phone that unfolds into a tablet).3. Performance as a FoundationPerformance isn't just a \"plus\"; it's a requirement. Google measures this through Core Web Vitals:LCP (Largest Contentful Paint): How fast the main content loads.INP (Interaction to Next Paint): How fast the UI reacts when a user clicks/taps.CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift): Ensuring elements don't \"jump around\" while loading (Visual Stability).4. Integration & ReliabilityA PWA bridges the gap between web and native:Standalone Experience: Once installed, it should look and feel like an app (no browser search bar, custom title bar colors).Offline-First: Use Service Workers to ensure the app doesn't break when the network fails.Platform Integration: Accessing the file system, contacts, or hardware APIs when available.Summary Checklist for your Projects:PillarAction ItemDesignPrioritize content first, then add layout layers.ResponsiveTest for \"Mini-Mode\" and foldable viewports.TechnicalImplement Feature Detection instead of UA sniffing.PerformanceOptimize for LCP, INP, and CLS scores.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
